## The null clause PASSES, and that retracts an earlier explanation of mine

    candidate_A/A            0.983029  ci95 [0.962283, 1.007947]
    control_A/A              0.995997  ci95 [0.979010, 1.016450]
    candidate_live_pair_A/A  1.011863  ci95 [0.990798, 1.037884]
    live_A/A                 0.994990  ci95 [0.980284, 1.013317]
    twice_widest_null_threshold = 1.078391   (was 1.258198 at 21 rounds)

At 21 rounds three of these four missed the 2% band (0.949, 0.939, 1.022) and I
reported that as a cold/warm ORDERING effect on the arms that re-factorize,
proposing to interleave the factorizations. That was wrong, and the evidence was
already in the same output: every one of those CIs spanned unity, so no bias had
been demonstrated at all. The nulls were IMPRECISE, not biased.

Raising the replicate count from 21 to 81 — changing nothing else, no schedule
change, no interleave — brought all four medians inside 2% and tightened the
widest-null threshold from 1.258 to 1.078. That is what an imprecision diagnosis
predicts and what a bias diagnosis does not. The interleave was never needed.

`all_null_ci95_span_unity=` is printed by this build so the distinction is on the
row rather than left to a reader's arithmetic. It is DIAGNOSTIC ONLY: no gate,
threshold or verdict was changed to obtain this pass.

